{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xd2ycUvNubUQwzFH3oGfbT","type":"tool_result","content":"  // `--template-repo`: the id sits in a reserved namespace that only the\n  // verified community load is granted — as an ordinary repo it is refused.\n  //\n  // Its mark is the Community M, re-baked on every release. That makes this\n  // the ONE id whose rendered frame drifts by design: the CODE is frozen at\n  // v1 (a layout / prop / reveal change is v2), but the mark PNG and the\n  // three preview assets are replaced per release. \"Same id renders the\n  // same\" holds per community release, not across them — nothing pins an\n  // old M, on purpose.\n  helloWorld: asTemplateId(\"@m0saic-community/hello-world/v1\"),\n  // The template-convention line this repo targets (m0saic 0.3.1): the checks\n  // hold its templates to the rules up to it; newer rules are advice until it moves.\n  conventions: \"0.3.1\",\n};\n\n/**\n * Named packs in this repo. Pack ids match the `{pack}` segment of member\n * template ids and each descriptor carries its publisher handle — with\n * publisher-scoped ids, `(publisher, pack)` is the real grouping key; the\n * per-entry `publisher` manifest field disambiguates if two publishers ever\n * ship the same pack name.\n175-  (`overlay.enable` / `window`), a scrim (`overlay.alpha`), a masked shape and an inset fill\n176-  (`placement`) are legitimate and never flagged. A **rounded or stroked card is NOT exempt\n177-  from 0.3.1 on** (2026-10-01): its corners change nothing about what a click on it does. A\n178:  repo held to 0.3.0 (its `frozen.manifest.json` release / `repo.conventions`) keeps the\n179-  exemption it shipped under — a tightened rule is a new demand, and lands with its line. What does exempt a\n180-  full-canvas rect is a **handle** — bind it to the prop a click on it should edit (the alpine\n181-  cards and the GitHub year card carry `theme.preset` / `preset`, so clicking the card is where\n182-  the theme gets changed). Templates already frozen are exempt. `bindingsDeclared` also accepts a colour prop that IS the document background\n183-  with neither a binding nor a declaration, because such a prop has no rect to bind.\n184-  - ⚠️ **The one case that needs a rect: a template COMPOSED INTO another.** The engine\n185-    lifts `backgroundColor` from the PRIMARY OUTPUT document only, so a child document's\n186-    own background is silently ignored (tracked at\n187-    (internal design history) — founder\n188-    owns the engine fix).\n189-  - **The declared escape** — an opt-in dev prop, NOT a rect in the default shape.\n190-    Defaulting it to `true` is ALSO how a template tells `canvasFill` that its rect is\n434-- **`behind`** — the lines it does not meet, with the rules that fail, oldest first.\n435-- **`shippedAt`** — the repo's own `frozen.manifest.json` `release`.\n436:- **`target`** — the repo's `repo.conventions` (e.g. `\"0.3.1\"`, 0.3.1): the line whose rules it has\n437-  adopted. Each template is held to the LOWER of its shipped line and the target; rules introduced after\n438-  that are lag (\"behind\"), never errors. A repo moves its target when its templates have adopted the\n439-  newer rules — like a crate's edition. Absent: held to every rule this CLI knows. A malformed target is\n440-  a load warning (`CONVENTIONS_TARGET`); one newer than the CLI knows is a note. The summary line reads\n441-  `conventions: meets 0.3.0 (targets 0.3.0).` and a lagging template says *\"the repo targets 0.3.0:\n442:  behind on catalogSidecar — not a defect, adopt them, then move repo.conventions.\"*\n443-- **`lagOnly`** — true when EVERY failure comes from a line newer than `shippedAt`. The footer then\n444-  says so explicitly: *\"Not a defect: these templates met the conventions of their day. Fix at the\n445-  next vN.\"*\n446-\n447-**A template is allowed to lag.** It met the conventions of its day; the rules moved; the fix is its\n448:next `vN`. **A repo is allowed to target an older line too** (`repo.conventions`): a new m0saic release\n449-never turns its build red — the freeze protects what shipped, the target protects a repo with no freeze\n450-(every third-party repo). What the rules guarantee is that **NEW** templates meet the CURRENT line — which is why the\n451-postures throw (founder: \"we need to be greedy about adding conventions because agents, especially\n452-non-frontier ones, won't\"). The exit code is still 1 on any error, because the status is informational\n453-and the message carries the meaning.\n454-\n455-`TEMPLATE_CONVENTION_SINCE` in `@m0saic/template-utils` is the map of rule → line. Everything\n456-pre-0.3.0 reads `0.2.0`: at that freeze the whole shipped fleet passed every rule then in force, so it\nShell cwd was reset to C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7dfecaa2-57f9-440a-a400-57b554bd1f45","uuid":"ed2c329f-3c87-4891-8fe3-74b5f1650edc","timestamp":"2026-10-06T21:23:45.335Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  // `--template-repo`: the id sits in a reserved namespace that only the\n  // verified community load is granted — as an ordinary repo it is refused.\n  //\n  // Its mark is the Community M, re-baked on every release. That makes this\n  // the ONE id whose rendered frame drifts by design: the CODE is frozen at\n  // v1 (a layout / prop / reveal change is v2), but the mark PNG and the\n  // three preview assets are replaced per release. \"Same id renders the\n  // same\" holds per community release, not across them — nothing pins an\n  // old M, on purpose.\n  helloWorld: asTemplateId(\"@m0saic-community/hello-world/v1\"),\n  // The template-convention line this repo targets (m0saic 0.3.1): the checks\n  // hold its templates to the rules up to it; newer rules are advice until it moves.\n  conventions: \"0.3.1\",\n};\n\n/**\n * Named packs in this repo. Pack ids match the `{pack}` segment of member\n * template ids an